<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theriso revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Therisos revolt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Theriso revolt was an uprising in the Cretan State in 1905, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleftherios-venizelos">Eleftherios Venizelos</a> from the village of Theriso near Chania. The rebels demanded union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and opposed the autocratic rule of the high commissioner, Prince George of Greece. The revolt led to Prince George's resignation in 1906 and raised Venizelos to political prominence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theriso-revolt.jpg" alt="Theriso revolt" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aeleftherios · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venizelos_Theriso2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the revolt of 1897 the Great Powers made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> an autonomous state under Ottoman suzerainty, with Prince George of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, second son of King George I, as High Commissioner and Venizelos as one of his ministers. The two soon quarrelled: Venizelos, chief author of the constitution, thought it gave the prince too much power, since the assembly met only every two years and only the prince could approve laws. George alone dealt with the Powers, hoping to win union through his family connections, while Venizelos thought union premature. When the Powers refused any change in February 1901, Venizelos offered his resignation; the prince dismissed him for insubordination on 20 March and later had him imprisoned after he answered press attacks in the newspaper Kyrix.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 1905 Venizelos, now for union at all costs, gathered Cretan leaders at Theriso, a village 14 km from Chania in the foothills of the White Mountains, guarded by narrow gorges full of caves; within twenty days some 7,000 sympathisers had come. On 23 March [O.S. 10 March] 1905 some 1,500 Cretans met there and proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>'s union with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The rebels set up a provisional government with its own stamps and newspaper, led by Venizelos, Konstantinos Foumis and Konstantinos Manos, and boycotted the elections; the new assembly at Chania nonetheless voted for union in April.</p><p>Prince George, with 1,100 gendarmes, declared martial law, and clashes near Chania left dead on both sides. Russian troops were the most active against the rebels, and in August the gunboat Khrabry shelled insurgents at Kasteli Panormos. The Greek government of Theodoros Deligiannis denounced the rising. With winter, debts and defections, Venizelos offered to lay down arms on honourable terms, and on 25 November 1905 the camp broke up under an amnesty in exchange for 700–800 guns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Powers then proposed reforms, among them a gendarmerie under a Greek officer, a Cretan force to allow foreign troops to leave, and equal rights for Christians and Muslims. Prince George opposed them, and he left the island for good on 25 September [O.S. 12 September] 1906. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandros-zaimis">Alexandros Zaimis</a> succeeded him, and a constituent assembly under Antonios Michelidakis gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> a new constitution. In 1908 the Cretan authorities proclaimed union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, which became official in 1913 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>. The revolt made Venizelos known across the Greek world; he later served seven times as Prime Minister of Greece between 1910 and 1933.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Theriso revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince George's autocratic style clashed with Eleftherios Venizelos, who led the <b>Therisos revolt</b> in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/state/cretan-state">Cretan State</a></li></ul></section>
